solnix: tzmon -- survive the ThinkPad false-128C thermal reading on the X1E.

SYMPTOM (ThinkPad X1 Extreme Gen5, 21DE001EUS): the live image boots to a
multi-user login, runs for ~40 s, then at the next thermal poll the console
prints and the machine halts (photographed 2026-10-18 18:50:51):

  tzmon: WARNING: tzmon: Thermal zone (\_TZ_.THM0) is critically hot (128 C);
  initiating rapid shutdown
  Press any key to reboot.

tzmon's _CRT handler calls mdboot(A_REBOOT, AD_HALT) when _TMP >= _CRT.

WHY 128 C IS NOT REAL. On this hardware \_TZ.THM0._CRT (TCRT) is exactly 128 C
(confirmed live on lixi: acpitz critical trip = 128000). _TMP returns a real
temperature via _C2K(sensor) OR exactly TCRT via two EC-level paths. The second
EC sensor level reads the 0x80 "sensor not populated" sentinel: lixi's
thinkpad_acpi reports `49 46 45 0 46 45 44 -128` (-128 = 0x80), and the DSDT
already special-cases 0x80 for the main sensor but not for the TSLx level
fields, so a level field at 0x80 drives _TMP straight to TCRT. illumos never ran
the ThinkPad EC/HKEY init Linux's thinkpad_acpi does, so it reads the raw
sentinel. A genuine overheat instead arrives as a rising _C2K() gradient and
crosses _HOT first; it never jumps straight to exactly _CRT from a cold idle.

TWO-PART FIX, both inside tzmon so they run before the first poll (no boot-order
race), and gated so non-ThinkPads are unaffected:

1. ThinkPad init (the platform shim): tzmon_attach finds the HKEY device
   (_HID LEN0268/LEN0068/IBM0068), installs a notify handler that drains MHKP
   and logs the real thermal-alarm events (0x6011/0x6012/0x6021/0x6022) with the
   EC's TSL0-TSL3 bitmaps, then calls MHKC(1) -- the init Linux does. Detach and
   the attach-failure paths undo it. No HKEY device -> nothing changes.

2. Sentinel guard (platform-general safety): tzmon_eval_zone refuses to shut
   down on a _TMP that reads back EXACTLY _CRT when the previous reading was not
   already at/approaching _CRT. That is the firmware's "sensor unreadable"
   signal, not a temperature. It logs once per zone and keeps polling; the EC's
   own thermal cutoff (below the OS) remains the real last line of defence, and a
   true sustained-critical reading still halts.

The maintainer's design goal is per-platform init discovered at boot. illumos's
standard mechanism for that is driver binding by ACPI _HID; part 1 is the first
such shim (it binds on the ThinkPad HKEY _HID, present only on ThinkPads). As
more platforms need init, each gets its own _HID-bound shim; part 2 is the
model-independent floor that keeps a bad sensor from powering a machine off.

